{"actor":{"display":"Evin Tunador","id":"evintunador@gmail.com","type":"human"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"86\t * Appended to a hook command for the two Gemini-derived CLIs.\n87\t *\n88\t * **Its original rationale was wrong, and is kept here as a warning.** It was\n89\t * introduced after `qwen -p \"...\"` failed to capture: the CLI fires its `Stop`\n90\t * hook and exits, and a hook doing a git-notes append was seen to die partway.\n91\t * The explanation recorded at the time -- that the redirection \"routes the\n92\t * command through a shell so the work runs as a grandchild that outlives the\n93\t * teardown\" -- does not survive reading either CLI's source. Both *always*\n94\t * invoke a hook as `bash -c \"<command>\"` whether or not it redirects, and\n95\t * `bash -c` with a single simple command `exec`s it rather than forking, so\n96\t * there is no grandchild and nothing is detached. All the redirect ever did\n97\t * was hide the capture's own output.\n98\t *\n99\t * The real cause of hooks dying was the timeout unit (see\n100\t * HOOK_TIMEOUT_MILLISECONDS): every hook was being SIGTERMed after 120ms.\n101\t *\n102\t * It is left in place only because removing it is a separate, untested change\n103\t * -- the headless `qwen -p` case has not been re-run since the timeout was\n104\t * fixed, and it may well no longer need this. Until then it keeps costing what\n105\t * it always cost: `cledger capture <source> --all` is the only way to see a\n106\t * capture's output, format-drift warnings included. Removing it is the first\n107\t * thing to try if a hook failure ever needs diagnosing again.\n108\t */\n109\tconst DETACH_SUFFIX = \" >/dev/null 2>&1\";\n110\t\n111\t/**\n112\t * Install command hooks into a Claude-Code-shaped `settings.json`.\n113\t *\n114\t * Three of the supported CLIs share this exact config format -- a top-level\n115\t * `hooks` object mapping an event name to an array of\n116\t * `{matcher?, hooks:[{type:\"command\", command, timeout}]}` definitions. That\n117\t * is not a coincidence: Gemini CLI ships a `gemini hooks migrate` command\n118\t * whose whole job is importing Claude Code's hook config, and Qwen Code\n119\t * inherited the same engine. Only the file path and the event names differ,\n120\t * so they are the parameters here.\n121\t */\n122\tasync function installJsonHooks(\n123\t  source: string,\n124\t  settingsPath: string,\n125\t  events: string[],\n126\t  options: { detach?: boolean; timeout: number } = { timeout: HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECONDS },\n127\t): Promise<string> {\n128\t  const settings: Record<string, unknown> = existsSync(settingsPath)\n129\t    ? (JSON.parse(await readFile(settingsPath, \"utf8\")) as Record<string, unknown>)\n130\t    : {};\n131\t  const command = (await hookCommand(source)) + (options.detach ? DETACH_SUFFIX : \"\");\n132\t  const hooks = (settings[\"hooks\"] ?? {}) as Record<string, ClaudeHookEntry[]>;\n133\t  let changed = false;\n134\t  let repaired = false;\n135\t  for (const event of events) {\n136\t    const existing = findCledgerHook(hooks[event], `hook ${source}`);\n137\t    if (!existing) {\n138\t      hooks[event] = [\n139\t        ...(hooks[event] ?? []),\n140\t        { hooks: [{ type: \"command\", command, timeout: options.timeout }] },\n141\t      ];\n142\t      changed = true;\n143\t      continue;\n144\t    }\n145\t    // An install already here is still repaired in place, because the timeout\n146\t    // this writes has been wrong before (see HOOK_TIMEOUT_MILLISECONDS) and a\n147\t    // user whose capture is being killed mid-write should be able to fix it by\n148\t    // re-running install rather than hand-editing JSON.\n149\t    if (existing.timeout !== options.timeout) {\n150\t      existing.timeout = options.timeout;\n151\t      changed = true;\n152\t      repaired = true;\n153\t    }\n154\t  }\n155\t  if (!changed) return `${source}: already installed (${settingsPath})`;\n156\t  if (repaired) {\n157\t    settings[\"hooks\"] = hooks;\n158\t  