{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01TEM9GXji5ftAwzf6zLUh5K","input":{"command":"git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\n0.22.0: exit quietly on a closed pipe, stop silencing hooks, hide by actor\n\nThree roadmap items that were each blocked on \"it wants its own change\".\n\n**`cledger export | head` no longer throws.** `process.stdout` had no `error`\nlistener, so the EPIPE Node raises when the reader goes away surfaced as an\nunhandled `error` event: a stack trace instead of output, on ordinary shell\nusage. EPIPE now exits 0 (the convention every head-friendly CLI follows) and\nany other stdout error reports as a message. The guard is installed for the\nwhole CLI, not just the JSON printers, because `log`'s human format and\n`conversations` write to the same stdout and broke the same way.\n\nThe second half was less obvious: `write()` reports EPIPE asynchronously, so a\nsynchronous print loop runs to completion after the reader is gone — the\nhandler fires only once the whole ledger has been buffered in memory. The bulk\nprinters now honour backpressure, which is what makes the exit prompt rather\nthan merely quiet. Tested by spawning the real binary and closing the pipe\nafter the first chunk; a fixture under 64KB passes without the fix, so the\ntest deliberately writes past it.\n\n**The Gemini/Qwen hook redirect is gone.** ` >/dev/null 2>&1` was added on the\ntheory that it detached the hook from the CLI's teardown. It never did — both\nCLIs run a hook as `bash -c`, which execs a single simple command rather than\nforking. The hooks were dying of the timeout unit, fixed earlier. All the\nredirect achieved was hiding the capture's own output, so a format-drift\nwarning had nowhere to print. `cledger install` now strips it from an existing\nhook in place, the same contract the timeout repair established: a setting\ncledger got wrong is fixable by re-running install. Only cledger's own hook,\nand only that exact trailing string, so another redirect survives.\n\n**Session machinery is hidden by actor, not by kind.** Hiding the four\nmachinery kinds wholesale earns its keep against telemetry, but it did not\ndistinguish whose action a record was — a slash command the human typed was\nhidden by the same rule as a token-count ping. A machinery event with a human\nactor now shows by default; `--with-state` still means show everything.\n\nThat needed the premise repaired first. \"`activity` already carries a real\nactor\" was half true: exactly one call site in the codebase passed `human`, so\nshipping the display change alone would have surfaced one record. Two\nattributions are corrected alongside it, both where the source states who\nacted — Gemini's `$rewindTo` is the person saying \"take that back\" (its\nsibling `snapshot_drop`, Gemini rewriting its own document, stays `system`, and\nthat difference is why the ledger records them as two things), and Qwen's\n`at_command` is a person naming a file, which was landing in\n`context_injection` with the same `system` actor as the harness's own\nbootstrap preamble.\n\nclaude-code's `queue-operation` is deliberately left alone. It looked like the\nsame fix, but the source does not say who queued it: measured across 40 local\ntranscripts, `enqueue` splits 126 harness-generated to 43 human-typed. The only\ndiscriminator is a content-shape heuristic, and getting it wrong files\nmachine-authored text under a named person's git identity — the same failure\nthis change is about, in the direction that does damage.\n\nAttribution changes are forward-only: `actor` is part of event identity, so a\nre-capture mints new ids for re-attributed records rather than deduping. Id\nchurn in one adapter's machinery records, not lost content, and no backfill —\nrewriting stored events would put two copies through `cat_sort_uniq`.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_013a944mewfSjMmV598jZzFb\nEOF\ngit log --oneline -1"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"fix-epipe-hook-detach-actor-display","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"e8e291d4d8b2ba087a0381e30793e0e67a873b35","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","seq":660},"id":"ev1-bf0b992f756e9b87a3e6f09ad5bebe012bc6f795c77c540e9dd13f125dc78b50","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-19T01:52:51.959Z","producer":{"model":"claude-opus-5","session_id":"224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.235","tool":"cledger","version":"0.22.0"},"raw":{"data":{"cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","effort":"high","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"fix-epipe-hook-detach-actor-display","isSidechain":false,"message":{"content":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01TEM9GXji5ftAwzf6zLUh5K","input":{"command":"git commit -q -F - <<'EOF'\n0.22.0: exit quietly on a closed pipe, stop silencing hooks, hide by actor\n\nThree roadmap items that were each blocked on \"it wants its own change\".\n\n**`cledger export | head` no longer throws.** `process.stdout` had no `error`\nlistener, so the EPIPE Node raises when the reader goes away surfaced as an\nunhandled `error` event: a stack trace instead of output, on ordinary shell\nusage. EPIPE now exits 0 (the convention every head-friendly CLI follows) and\nany other stdout error reports as a message. The guard is installed for the\nwhole CLI, not just the JSON printers, because `log`'s human format and\n`conversations` write to the same stdout and broke the same way.\n\nThe second half was less obvious: `write()` reports EPIPE asynchronously, so a\nsynchronous print loop runs to completion after the reader is gone — the\nhandler fires only once the whole ledger has been buffered in memory. The bulk\nprinters now