{"actor":{"id":"claude-opus-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"caller":{"type":"direct"},"id":"toolu_01XJYfth2x2wQquxuszN7U73","input":{"command":"gh pr create --title \"0.22.0: exit quietly on a closed pipe, stop silencing hooks, hide by actor\" --body \"$(cat <<'EOF'\nThree roadmap items, each of which had been deferred as \"wants its own change\".\n\n## 1. `cledger export | head` no longer dies with an unhandled EPIPE\n\n`process.stdout` had no `error` listener, so the EPIPE Node raises when the reader goes away surfaced as an unhandled `error` event — a stack trace instead of output, for ordinary shell usage. Reproduces on any ledger past the 64KB pipe buffer; small ones hide it because the whole payload lands in the buffer before `head` exits.\n\nEPIPE now exits 0 (the convention every `head`-friendly CLI follows); any other stdout error reports as a message and exits 1. The guard covers the whole CLI rather than just the three JSON printers, since `log`'s human format and `conversations` write to the same stdout and broke identically.\n\n**The second half was less obvious.** `write()` reports EPIPE *asynchronously*, so a synchronous print loop runs to completion after the reader is already gone — the handler fires, but only once the entire ledger has been buffered in memory. The bulk printers now honour backpressure (`await once(stdout, \"drain\")`), which is what makes the exit prompt rather than merely quiet.\n\nVerified against a 323KB ledger: `cledger export | head -1` → exit 0, zero bytes on stderr. Previously threw every time.\n\n## 2. The Gemini/Qwen hook redirect is removed\n\n` >/dev/null 2>&1` was added on the theory that it detached the hook from the CLI's teardown. It never did — both CLIs invoke a hook as `bash -c`, which `exec`s a single simple command rather than forking. The hooks were dying of the timeout-unit bug, fixed earlier. All the redirect ever achieved was hiding the capture's own output, so a format-drift warning had nowhere to print.\n\n`cledger install` now also **strips it from an existing hook in place**, the same contract the timeout repair established: a setting cledger itself got wrong should be fixable by re-running install, not by hand-editing JSON. Only cledger's own hook is touched, and only when the command ends in that exact string.\n\n*Still unverified:* the original headless `qwen -p` case needs a live Qwen session to re-run. The reasoning says the redirect was never what saved it, but nobody has watched it since.\n\n## 3. Session machinery is hidden by actor, not by kind\n\nHiding the four machinery kinds wholesale earns its keep against telemetry (one real Qwen session: 57 `ui_telemetry` records against 45 events of content), but it did not distinguish *whose* action a record was — a slash command the human typed was hidden by the same rule as a token-count ping. A machinery event with a `human` actor now shows by default; `--with-state` still means show everything, and capture/export are untouched.\n\n**The premise needed repair first.** \"`activity` already carries a real actor\" turned out half true — exactly one call site in the codebase passed `human`, so shipping the display change alone would have surfaced one record type and nothing else. Two attributions are corrected alongside it, both where the source *states* who acted:\n\n- **Gemini's `$rewindTo`** → `human`. Its sibling `snapshot_drop` (Gemini quietly rewriting its own document) stays `system` — that difference is the entire reason the ledger records them as two things.\n- **Qwen's `at_command`** → `human`. It was landing in `context_injection` with the identical `system` actor as `agent_bootstrap`, the harness's own preamble that nobody typed. `contextInjectionDraft` grew an actor parameter to match `activityDraft`.\n\n**claude-code's `queue-operation` is deliberately left alone.** It looked like the same fix, but the source does not say who queued it. Measured across 40 local transcripts: `enqueue` splits 126 harness-generated to 43 human-typed, `remove` 70 to 16. The only discriminator is a content-shape heuristic (harness entries arrive wrapped in a `task-notification` envelope), and getting it wrong files machine-authored text under a named person's git identity — the failure this change is about, in the direction that actually does damage. Documented in the roadmap; revisit if claude-code ever labels the entry's origin.\n\n**Forward-only.** `actor.type`/`actor.id` are part of event identity, so re-capturing an already-captured session mints new ids for re-attributed records rather than deduping. Id churn in one adapter's machinery records, not lost content. No backfill — rewriting stored events would put two copies through `cat_sort_uniq` on the next merge with any peer.\n\n## Testing\n\n`npm test`: **223 tests, 222 pass, 0 fail, 1 skipped.**\n\nNew coverage:\n- `src/test/cli-epipe.test.ts` — spawns the real binary and closes the pipe after the first chunk (an in-process call has no pipe to break). Confirmed non-vacuous: 3 of 4 fail with the guard removed. Also pins that the guard does *not* swallow ordinary command failures.\n- `src/test/display-filter.test.ts` — one event per (kind, actor) pair through the real CLI; asserts the default view, `--with-state`, `--kind`, and that `export` stays lossless.\n- `install.test.ts` — fresh installs write no redirect; re-running strips a stale one and is idempotent; a redirect that is not cledger's own survives.\n- Adapter tests for the Gemini rewind/snapshot-drop actor split and the Qwen `at_command`/`agent_bootstrap` split.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n\nhttps://claude.ai/code/session_013a944mewfSjMmV598jZzFb\nEOF\n)\" 2>&1 | tail -5"},"name":"Bash","type":"tool_use"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"fix-epipe-hook-detach-actor-display","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"e8e291d4d8b2ba087a0381e30793e0e67a873b35","repo":"git@github.com:ev