 type here, unlike claude-code where a tool result rides inside a\n * `user` message — hence three convertible types rather than two.\n */\nconst CONVERTIBLE_LINE_TYPES = new Set([\"user\", \"assistant\", \"tool_result\"]);\n\n/**\n * Every top-level record type Qwen Code writes, taken verbatim from its own\n * `RECORD_TYPES` set. A line whose type is outside this set is genuinely new\n * upstream content and routes to the drift path — preserved raw, warned\n * about, upgradeable later by `cledger renormalize`.\n */\nconst RECORD_TYPES = new Set([\"user\", \"assistant\", \"tool_result\", \"system\"]);\n\n/**\n * How a `system` line's `subtype` maps onto a record kind.\n *\n * Qwen Code funnels everything that is not a turn through `system`, so the\n * subtype is the real discriminator; the names below are its own\n * `KNOWN_RECORD_SUBTYPES`. The split follows the same reasoning the\n * claude-code adapter applies to its top-level types: a declaration that\n * holds until restated is `session_state`, material the harness put in front\n * of the model is `context_injection`, and intermediate file state is\n * `file_snapshot`.\n *\n * Anything not listed — including a subtype a future Qwen Code adds — falls\n * through to `activity`, which is the honest default: `system` is a type this\n * adapter knows, so preserving it raw-only and warning about drift would be a\n * false alarm, but claiming to know that an unseen subtype is *state* would\n * be a guess. \"Something happened, here are its fields verbatim\" is true of\n * all of them.\n */\nconst SESSION_STATE_SUBTYPES = new Set([\n  \"custom_title\",\n  \"session_source\",\n  \"parent_session\",\n  \"goal_state\",\n]);\nconst CONTEXT_INJECTION_SUBTYPES = new Set([\n  \"at_command\",\n  \"agent_bootstrap\",\n  \"agent_launch_prompt\",\n]);\nconst FILE_SNAPSHOT_SUBTYPES = new Set([\"attribution_snapshot\", \"file_history_snapshot\"]);\n\n/**\n * `system` subtypes the person at the keyboard drove, rather than the harness.\n *\n * They stay `activity` — Qwen records `sentToModel: false` on them, so they\n * are not turns in the conversation the model saw — but the actor is the human,\n * because a human typed them. Verified on a live session: a `slash_command`\n * record carries the literal `rawCommand` (\"/chat save smoke-test\"), which is\n * keystrokes, not harness bookkeeping. Filing that under `system` would be the\n * ledger recording a person's action as the machine's.\n *\n * This matches the rule the gemini-cli adapter already states for the same\n * class of thing: harness-authored preambles are `system`, slash commands are\n * the human's.\n */\nconst HUMAN_DRIVEN_SUBTYPES = new Set([\"slash_command\"]);\n\n/**\n * Envelope fields every Qwen Code line carries. Dropped from the structured\n * `content` of record events because they are already on the event itself\n * (`occurred_at`, `producer.session_id`, `producer.source_version`) or in\n * `raw.data` — repeating them in `content` would put one fact in three places\n * with three chances to disagree. Mirrors claude-code's `ENVELOPE_KEYS`.\n */\nconst ENVELOPE_KEYS = new Set([\n  \"type\",\n  \"subtype\",\n  \"uuid\",\n  \"parentUuid\",\n  \"sessionId\",\n  \"timestamp\",\n  \"cwd\",\n  \"version\",\n  \"gitBranch\",\n  \"provenance\",","filePath":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger/src/adapters/qwen-code.ts","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":684},"type":"text"},"type":"user","userType":"external","uuid":"bfec9c68-c995-48b9-bef2-00486a801cc6","version":"2.1.233"},"format":"claude-code-jsonl/1"},"recorded_at":"2026-08-19T01:54:01.133Z","schema":"conversation-ledger/v1"}
{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"1\t/**\n2\t * opencode adapter.\n3\t *\n4\t * Unlike claude-code and codex, opencode has no append-only transcript file:\n5\t * sessions live in a mutable SQLite database at\n6\t * `~/.local/share/opencode/opencode.db`. Rather than read that database, this\n7\t * adapter shells out to opencode's own `opencode export <sessionID>`, which\n8\t * emits `{info, messages: [{info, parts}]}` JSON. Three reasons:\n9\t *\n10\t *  1. cledger ships with no runtime dependencies and supports Node >= 20;\n11\t *     `node:sqlite` would force both to change.\n12\t *  2. opencode's schema is visibly mid-migration (an empty `session_message`\n13\t *     table sits alongside the populated `message`/`part` tables it is\n14\t *     evidently meant to replace). The export shape is the interface opencode\n15\t *     documents; the tables are not.\n16\t *  3. The same database holds `credential.value` and `account.access_token`.\n17\t *     Reading conversations should not require opening the file that stores\n18\t *     the user's API tokens.\n19\t *\n20\t * `--sanitize` is deliberately *not* passed to the export: cledger has its\n21\t * own redaction stack, and letting opencode rewrite content would make event\n22\t * ids depend on opencode's version.\n23\t */\n24\timport { spawn } from \"node:child_process\";\n25\timport { mkdtemp, open, readFile, rm } from \"node:fs/promises\";\n26\timport { tmpdir } from \"node:os\";\n27\timport { join } from \"node:path\";\n28\timport { findRepo, gitUserIdentity, type GitUserIdentity } from \"../git.js\";\n29\timport { appendEvents } from \"../store.js\";\n30\timport type { Actor, EventDraft, EvidenceEvent, ProducerAgentContext } from \"../schema.js\";\n31\timport { packageVersion, readCursor, writeCursor } from \"./common.js\";\n32\timport {\n33\t  countUnrecognized,\n34\t  mergeCaptureResult,\n35\t  unrecognizedDraft,\n36\t  warnUnrecognized,\n37\t  type CaptureResult,\n38\t} from \"./drift.js\";\n39\timport { activityDraft, type RecordContext } from \"./records.js\";\n40\t\n41\tconst RAW_FORMAT = \"opencode-export-json/1\";\n42\t\n43\tconst CURSOR_FIELD = \"parts\";\n44\t\n45\t/**\n46\t * Part types carrying visible conversation content.\n47\t *\n48\t * `reasoning` belongs here, and that is a real difference from codex: codex's\n49\t * reasoning items are provider-encrypted (`encrypted_content`) and can only\n50\t * ever be preserved opaquely as `reasoning`-kind events, whereas opencode\n51\t * stores reasoning as plain `text`. So it converts to an ordinary\n52\t * `thinking` block inside a `conversation_turn`, exactly as claude-code's\n53\t * thinking blocks do — no opacity marker, nothing withheld.\n54\t */\n55\tconst CONVERTIBLE_PART_TYPES = new Set([\"text\", \"reasoning\", \"tool\"]);\n56\t\n57\t/**\n58\t * Part types recorded as `activity` rather than as turns.\n59\t *\n60\t * `step-start` and `step-finish` carry no prose, which is why they were once\n61\t * dropped, but they carry the two things nothing else in the export does: the\n62\t * git snapshot hash opencode took at each step boundary, and the token counts,\n63\t * cost, and finish reason for the step. The snapshot hash in particular is a\n64\t * real handle on intermediate repository state — the closest opencode analogue\n65\t * to Claude Code's file history.\n66\t *\n67\t * `patch` points into opencode's private snapshot store, and the diff it names\n68\t * is usually already in git. It is recorded anyway, for the same reason the\n69\t * file-history pointers are: \"usually already in git\" is not \"always\", and a\n70\t * pointer with provenance beats a hole.\n71\t *\n72\t * Part types opencode's binary references but no session on hand exercises —\n73\t * `file`, `agent`, `snapshot`, `todo` — are still left off every list here.\n74\t * Guessing at their shape would discard content on the first session that used\n75\t * one; leaving them out routes them through the drift path, which preserves\n76\t * them raw, warns, and lets `cledger renormalize` upgrade them later.\n77\t */\n78\tconst ACTIVITY_PART_TYPES = new Set([\"step-start\", \"step-finish\", \"patch\"]);\n79\t\n80\t/**\n81\t * Tool-call states that will never change again. A `pending`/`running` part\n82\t * is still being written; capturing it would bake a half-finished tool call\n83\t * into an immutable event id. The capture loop skips unsettled parts *and*\n84\t * holds the cursor back at the first one, so the finished version is picked\n85\t * up on the next capture rather than being skipped forever.\n86\t */\n87\tconst SETTLED_TOOL_STATUSES = new Set([\"completed\", \"error\"]);\n88\t\n89\t/** Payload the installed opencode plugin sends on stdin (see install.ts). */\n90\tinterface OpencodeHookPayload {\n91\t  session_id?: string;\n92\t  cwd?: string;\n93\t  hook_event_name?: string;\n94\t}\n95\t\n96\tinterface OpencodeSessionInfo {\n97\t  id?: string;\n98\t  /** Set on subagent sessions; those are skipped (see captureOpencodeExport). */\n99\t  parentID?: string;\n100\t  directory?: string;","tool_use_id":"toolu_01S7m9f8222h9Fk5NCxxWLpr","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"fix-epipe-hook-detach-actor-display","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"e8e291d4d8b2ba087a0381e30793e0e67a873b35","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d#agent:a61781e63a0a098a3","parent":"claude-code:224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","seq":58},"id":"ev1-3505c50343b48fd48b2efcc47329d0fcb19a24aaf1c5d837ffd6026f57ca477e","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-16T11:23:50.049Z","producer":{"session_id":"224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.233","tool":"cledger","version":"0.22.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a61781e63a0a098a3","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","entrypoint":"cl