{"actor":{"type":"system"},"content":{"blocks":[{"content":"300\t  corpus convention means assembled fixture bytes never reach a committed\n301\t  file, and the other spans lived in shell commands and intermediate edit\n302\t  states that were rewritten before committing. The mechanism is sound but\n303\t  its hit rate on real backlogs is unproven, and it adds a scan-time\n304\t  dependency on remote-reachability computation. Revisit if a backlog ever\n305\t  shows committed-content spans; `cledger review` clearing 15 spans in a\n306\t  minute weakens the case for more machinery.\n307\t- **`cledger review` shows a context window, not the conversation** — the\n308\t  0.21.0 reviewer renders the flagged string with ±600 chars (scrollable,\n309\t  the whole string is available). A richer version would page through the\n310\t  *conversation* around the event — prior and following turns, like reading\n311\t  the transcript — which needs event-sequence pagination keyed on\n312\t  `conversation.seq` and a real layout pass. Deferred until the per-span\n313\t  flow proves insufficient; most verdicts so far need only the surrounding\n314\t  string.\n315\t- **The `>/dev/null 2>&1` on the Gemini and Qwen hooks is probably now\n316\t  pointless, and costs visibility** — it was added after `qwen -p \"...\"` failed\n317\t  to capture, on the theory that the redirect \"routes the command through a\n318\t  shell so the work outlives the teardown\". That theory is wrong: both CLIs\n319\t  *always* invoke a hook as `bash -c \"<command>\"`, and `bash -c` `exec`s a\n320\t  single simple command rather than forking, so nothing was ever detached. The\n321\t  actual cause of hooks dying was the timeout unit below. The suffix survives\n322\t  only because removing it is an untested change — headless `qwen -p` has not\n323\t  been re-run without it since the timeout was fixed — and until then it keeps\n324\t  costing what it always cost: the capture cannot print drift warnings where\n325\t  you would see them, so `cledger capture <source> --all` is the only way to\n326\t  watch one. Removing it is the first thing to try; it is also what made the\n327\t  timeout bug take a full debugging session to find.\n328\t- **`cledger log` hides human-driven records by default** — the\n329\t  session-machinery kinds (`session_state`, `activity`, `context_injection`,\n330\t  `file_snapshot`) are hidden unless you pass `--with-state`. That default\n331\t  earns its keep against telemetry: one real Qwen session emitted 57\n332\t  `ui_telemetry` records against 45 events of actual content, and an unfiltered\n333\t  default would bury the conversation. But it does not distinguish *whose*\n334\t  action a record was, so a slash command the human typed is hidden by the same\n335\t  rule as a token-count ping. Capture is unaffected — everything is recorded\n336\t  and `cledger export` is lossless, these flags only change what is displayed —\n337\t  but the display default is arguably drawing the line in the wrong place. The\n338\t  fix worth trying is to key the default on the actor rather than the kind:\n339\t  show human-driven records, keep harness and agent bookkeeping behind the\n340\t  flag. `activity` already carries a real actor for exactly this reason.\n341\t- **Gemini CLI's version is not in its session file** — claude-code and\n342\t  qwen-code stamp the CLI version onto every line, so `producer.source_version`\n343\t  comes for free. Gemini stamps none, and neither the hook payload nor the\n344\t  session header carries one, so gemini-cli events have no `source_version`\n345\t  unless a caller passes it. Reading it off the installed binary would be\n346\t  wrong for a backfill of an old session, and guessing is worse than omitting.\n347\t  Fixing it properly means asking Gemini to record it, or accepting a\n348\t  capture-time \"the binary on PATH said 0.53.1\" annotation that is explicitly\n349\t  about the capture rather than the session.\n350\t- **`timeout` means different units in Claude Code than in the CLIs that copied\n351\t  its hook format** — Claude Code reads a command hook's `timeout` as seconds;\n352\t  Gemini CLI and Qwen Code pass the same field straight to `setTimeout`, so\n353\t  theirs is milliseconds (`Hook timed out after ${timeout}ms`,\n354\t  `DEFAULT_HOOK_TIMEOUT = 6e4`). `cledger install` wrote Claude's `120` into all\n355\t  three, so on Gemini and Qwen every hook was SIGTERMed after 120 *milliseconds*\n356\t  — less than a cold capture takes. The symptom was a CLI reporting\n357\t  `Hook(s) [...] failed` on nearly every turn while its events sometimes still\n358\t  landed, because whether the append finished before the signal was a race.\n359\t  Fixed by writing each CLI's own unit, and `install` now repairs an existing","tool_use_id":"toolu_011bM7CM3yxoUU3sQv2GCqNY","type":"tool_result"}],"role":"user"},"context":{"branch":"fix-epipe-hook-detach-actor-display","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","head":"e8e291d4d8b2ba087a0381e30793e0e67a873b35","repo":"git@github.com:evintunador/conversation-ledger.git"},"conversation":{"id":"claude-code:224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d#agent:a61781e63a0a098a3","parent":"claude-code:224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","seq":75},"id":"ev1-449bf713cd238ff815f900472cd69b0448fdc69b74a68104772884c5eb5b5f6f","kind":"conversation_turn","occurred_at":"2026-08-16T11:24:11.884Z","producer":{"session_id":"224d1239-00ba-4ddb-8117-5a487d569b0d","source":"claude-code","source_version":"2.1.233","tool":"cledger","version":"0.22.0"},"raw":{"data":{"agentId":"a61781e63a0a098a3","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/conversation-ledger","entrypoint":"cli","gitBranch":"fix-epipe-hook-detach-actor-display","isSidechain":true,"message":{"content":[{"content":"300\t  corpus convention means assembled fixture bytes never reach a committed\n301\t  file, and the other spans lived in shell commands and intermediate edit\n302\t  states that were re